*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   SCREEN MOUSE RETURNING m {THEN ... ELSE ...}
*
*   is compiled into
*
*   call !screen_mouse(ret, status)
*
*     ret      the last mouse event the library read, as fields: 1 the kind (press, release, drag or wheel),
*              2 the button (1 left, 2 middle, 3 right; 4 for a drag with none held; up or down for the wheel),
*              3 the column and 4 the row of the cell it was over, from 0 as in @(x, y), and 5 any of s, a and
*              c for the shift, alt and control keys that were down, in that order
*     status   0 there has been one; 1 there has not (ELSE), or there is no terminal
*
*   A program that handles the mouse itself calls SCREEN KEY, which answers mouse when the event was one, and
*   then SCREEN MOUSE.  Forms, menus and dialogues take clicks without the program's help.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine screen_mouse(ret, status)
$catalog !screen_mouse

   ret = ''
   status = 1
   if system(25) then return                  ;* a phantom has no terminal
   call $sl_mouse('get', ret)
   if ret # '' then status = 0

   return
end

* END-CODE
